      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Practice -ar",
        "body": "A Spanish verb names an action. To conjugate means to change it for the person who acts. An infinitive is the dictionary form, such as cantar. The stem is what remains after -ar, and the ending is the part we add. Cantar belongs to the -ar family.\n\nIn cantar, cant- is the stem. Cant- plus -o makes yo canto; plus -as makes tú cantas; plus -a makes él canta. Hablar follows the same pattern: hablo, hablas, habla.\n\nPractice: Split estudiar into its stem and ending. Write yo estudio, tú estudias, and él estudia. Then write one sentence with Yo estudio.\n\nCommon mistake: Do not write yo cantar. That is the dictionary form; yo canto tells who acts."
      }
    ],
    "summary": ""
  },
